194
00:07:53,520 --> 00:07:56,520
And that will take up the remainder of the podcast.

195
00:07:56,520 --> 00:07:58,520
So let's get started, shall we?

196
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:00,520
Scoutmastership in seven minutes

197
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:02,520
or less.

198
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:12,520
I want to talk about two types

199
00:08:12,520 --> 00:08:15,520
of questions that I get quite often,

200
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:17,520
because they really share a common answer,

201
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:19,520
and that is, you know,

202
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:21,520
the context of scouting.

203
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:23,520
Let me see if I can explain what I mean.

204
00:08:23,520 --> 00:08:25,520
I'll get a question like this:

205
00:08:25,520 --> 00:08:27,520
What if our patrol leaders council decided

206
00:08:27,520 --> 00:08:29,520
to cancel all the troop meetings

207
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:30,520
for the next two months?

208
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:32,520
I mean that is boy-led, right.

209
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:34,520
Or what if our patrol leaders council voted

210
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:37,520
and they decide that the next six months

211
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:39,520
of trips we're going to take

212
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:41,520
are all going to be visits

213
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:43,520
to the local amusement parks?

214
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:45,520
I mean we are a boy-led.

215
00:08:45,520 --> 00:08:46,520
If they make the decision,

216
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:48,520
we should follow it, right?

217
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:50,520
However, our patrol leaders council decided

218
00:08:50,520 --> 00:08:52,520
that, instead of having elections,

219
00:08:52,520 --> 00:08:54,520
the adults should appoint

220
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:56,520
the senior patrol leader.

221
00:08:56,520 --> 00:08:59,520
I mean they are making the decision

222
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:01,520
and we are youth-led,

223
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:04,520
so we should accept that decision, right?

224
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:06,520
I think the first point I want to make-

225
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:08,520
and I don't want to be really pedantic about this,

226
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:10,520
but let me observe

227
00:09:10,520 --> 00:09:12,520
that we don't work with boys,

228
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:15,520
We work with scouts, right?

229
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:17,520
I know it sounds like I'm splitting hairs,

230
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:20,520
but there's actually a really important distinction

231
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:23,520
there that answers this question.

232
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:26,520
We don't have boys leading anything.

233
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:29,520
We don't have boys in our patrol leaders council,

234
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:31,520
We only have scouts,

235
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:33,520
And I say this because it's important

236
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:36,520
to consistently express and reinforce the message

237
00:09:36,520 --> 00:09:38,520
that scouts lead themselves

238
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:41,520
within the context of the scouting program.

239
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:43,520
That's why I think it's important

240
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:46,520
to think of them as scouts rather than boys.

241
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:48,520
I mentioned this kind of thing in a recent podcast,

242
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:50,520
but I think it bears repeating.

243
00:09:50,520 --> 00:09:53,520
The kind of recursive loop of logic

244
00:09:53,520 --> 00:09:56,520
that says anything that the boys decide

245
00:09:56,520 --> 00:09:59,520
is therefore youth leadership.

246
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:03,520
it's missing a few important foundational concepts.

247
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:05,520
By now. you've probably grown tired

248
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:07,520
of my frequent sports analogies,

249
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:09,520
but I think it really works here.

250
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:12,520
Does a basketball team get to vote on the rules

251
00:10:12,520 --> 00:10:14,520
or the constraints of the game?

252
00:10:14,520 --> 00:10:16,520
Well, no, they don't have a vote on that.

253
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:18,520
You have the rules and constraints

254
00:10:18,520 --> 00:10:20,520
and the context of the game

255
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:22,520
and you play the game with basketball.

256
00:10:22,520 --> 00:10:25,520
So the purpose and achievement that we get out of scouting

257
00:10:25,520 --> 00:10:28,520
doesn't come from simple, unfettered freedom

258
00:10:28,520 --> 00:10:31,520
that we can make any decision that we want to make.

259
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:34,520
There are constraints and contexts

260
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:37,520
that define the types of decisions

261
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:39,520
that scouts make.

262
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:41,520
In scouting. our defining constraint

263
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:43,520
is the nature of the program

264
00:10:43,520 --> 00:10:45,520
and the policies and procedures

265
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:47,520
that help define the nature of the program.

266
00:10:47,520 --> 00:10:49,520
And these constraints aren't negative,

267
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:51,520
not by a long shot.

268
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:53,520
They're actually very positive

269
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:55,520
and they're inspiring.

270
00:10:55,520 --> 00:10:57,520
I would say that creativity and innovation

271
00:10:57,520 --> 00:10:59,520
comes from, in part,

272
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:02,520
the constraints of the question, don't they?

273
00:11:02,520 --> 00:11:05,520
So we work within a set of constraints

274
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:07,520
and in a context called scouting.

275
00:11:07,520 --> 00:11:09,520
We're not led by boys,

276
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:11,520
we're led by scouts.

277
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:13,520
The decisions that they make

278
00:11:13,520 --> 00:11:15,520
have to be based on the context and the constraints.

279
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:17,520
That's just the rules of the game.

