00:11:17,520 --> 00:11:19,520
I said there were two questions that I get frequently

281
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:21,520
that have the same answer.

282
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:23,520
so, along the same lines,

283
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:25,520
I'm frequently asked how I'd react

284
00:11:25,520 --> 00:11:27,520
if, as I suggest, scouts are having, say,

285
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,520
and how they make up their own patrols.

286
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:31,520
What if there's a scout

287
00:11:31,520 --> 00:11:33,520
that nobody wants in their patrol?

288
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:35,520
And the answer is always the same:

289
00:11:35,520 --> 00:11:38,520
As scouts were constrained,

290
00:11:38,520 --> 00:11:40,520
right to act within

291
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:42,520
the oath and law

292
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:44,520
in everything,

293
00:11:44,520 --> 00:11:46,520
No other rules are required.

294
00:11:46,520 --> 00:11:48,520
We don't want to make other rules.

295
00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:50,520
All we need to do is be champions

296
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:52,520
of the scout oath and law.

297
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:54,520
Scouts follow the oath and law.

298
00:11:54,520 --> 00:11:56,520
They're not concerned with popularity

299
00:11:56,520 --> 00:11:58,520
or who they like and who they don't like.

300
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:00,520
They're concerned with serving

301
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:02,520
their fellow scouts.

302
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:04,520
Now, sometimes boys

303
00:12:04,520 --> 00:12:06,520
need to think through

304
00:12:06,520 --> 00:12:08,520
what it means to be

305
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:10,520
a scout.

306
00:12:10,520 --> 00:12:12,520
So let's say that a boy tells us:

307
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:14,520
I don't want to be in a patrol with that guy.

308
00:12:14,520 --> 00:12:16,520
He's just kind of a pain.

309
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:18,520
So your answer would be:

310
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:20,520
well, what part of the scout oath and law

311
00:12:20,520 --> 00:12:22,520
describes rejecting a fellow scout

312
00:12:22,520 --> 00:12:24,520
because you think he's a pain?

313
00:12:24,520 --> 00:12:26,520
So every boy,

314
00:12:26,520 --> 00:12:28,520
no matter how painful or unpopular,

315
00:12:28,520 --> 00:12:30,520
gets to be

316
00:12:30,520 --> 00:12:32,520
a scout.

317
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:34,520
We accept him as he is

318
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:36,520
when we practice the scout oath and law.

319
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:38,520
Now I understand that there are

320
00:12:38,520 --> 00:12:40,520
friendships between scouts

321
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:42,520
and those friendships are very important.

322
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:44,520
But there's also something broader that happens

323
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:46,520
when we practice the scout oath and law:

324
00:12:46,520 --> 00:12:48,520
that a scout is a friend

325
00:12:48,520 --> 00:12:50,520
to every other scout.

326
00:12:50,520 --> 00:12:52,520
That certainly doesn't mean

327
00:12:52,520 --> 00:12:54,520
that every other scout is their favorite.

328
00:12:54,520 --> 00:12:56,520
But if the oath and law is to mean

329
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:58,520
anything at all,

330
00:12:58,520 --> 00:13:00,520
no scout certainly is an enemy

331
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:02,520
of another scout

332
00:13:02,520 --> 00:13:04,520
And no scout is

333
00:13:04,520 --> 00:13:06,520
thereby going to be deemed unpopular.

334
00:13:06,520 --> 00:13:08,520
Now this all sounds kind of glib

335
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:10,520
and unrealistic, but I know,

336
00:13:10,520 --> 00:13:12,520
just as you do, that boys sometimes

337
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:14,520
find it challenging

338
00:13:14,520 --> 00:13:16,520
to be scouts.

339
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:18,520
So that's where it's our responsibility:

340
00:13:18,520 --> 00:13:20,520
to champion the oath and law

341
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:22,520
as the basis of everything

342
00:13:22,520 --> 00:13:24,520
that we do and all the decisions

343
00:13:24,520 --> 00:13:26,520
that they make. If you work at this,

344
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:28,520
there's no questions

345
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:30,520
about popularity. There's

346
00:13:30,520 --> 00:13:32,520
no room for marginalizing a scout

347
00:13:32,520 --> 00:13:34,520
based on whether other boys

348
00:13:34,520 --> 00:13:36,520
like him or not.

349
00:13:36,520 --> 00:13:38,520
Boys may do that,

350
00:13:38,520 --> 00:13:40,520
but scouts never would.

351
00:13:40,520 --> 00:13:42,520
So we

352
00:13:42,520 --> 00:13:44,520
champion the scout oath and law.

353
00:13:44,520 --> 00:13:46,520
We speak about it all the time.

354
00:13:46,520 --> 00:13:48,520
We talk with our patrol leaders council.

355
00:13:48,520 --> 00:13:50,520
We discuss how the oath and law applies

356
00:13:50,520 --> 00:13:52,520
to everything that they do and all the decisions

357
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:54,520
that they make. We work through some scenarios.

358
00:13:54,520 --> 00:13:56,520
I would sit down and ask my patrol

359
00:13:56,520 --> 00:13:58,520
leaders council and I have frequently

360
00:13:58,520 --> 00:14:00,520
what if nobody wants a scout in their patrol?

361
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:02,520
How are you going to deal with that

362
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:04,520
And the ensuing discussion?

363
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:06,520
we're able to talk about the oath and law.

364
00:14:06,520 --> 00:14:08,520
We're able to talk about the difference just being

365
00:14:08,520 --> 00:14:10,520
a boy somewhere and being a scout,

366
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:12,520
and I'm able to encourage them

367
00:14:12,520 --> 00:14:14,520
to make it a point of pride in their troop

368
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:16,520
that everyone is accepted

369
00:14:16,520 --> 00:14:18,520
and treated as a friend

370
00:14:18,520 --> 00:14:20,520
And that way there's never a question of leaving

371
00:14:20,520 --> 00:14:22,520
anybody out when it comes time

372
00:14:22,520 --> 00:14:24,520
to reorganize patrols or to elect

373
00:14:24,520 --> 00:14:26,520
leaders or something like that.

374
00:14:26,520 --> 00:14:28,520
We create a culture of the scout

375
00:14:28,520 --> 00:14:30,520
oath and law. So two questions

376
00:14:30,520 --> 00:14:32,520
with a common answer.

377
00:14:32,520 --> 00:14:34,520
We work within the context of

378
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:36,520
scouting and that disallows

379
00:14:36,520 --> 00:14:38,520
these theoretically kind of

380
00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:40,520
nutty decisions that a group

381
00:14:40,520 --> 00:14:42,520
of boys might make, but

382
00:14:42,520 --> 00:14:44,520
scouts never would. And we work

383
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:46,520
within the context of the scout

384
00:14:46,520 --> 00:14:48,520
oath and law. It describes

385
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:50,520
our attitude and how we

386
00:14:50,520 --> 00:14:52,520
do things. So,

387
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:54,520
within the context of the oath and law,

388
00:14:54,520 --> 00:14:56,520
it's possible for a scout

389
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:58,520
to be unpopular, to have no friends,

390
00:14:58,520 --> 00:15:00,520
And that's what we aspire to.

391
00:15:00,520 --> 00:15:02,520
There's bumps, setbacks and challenges

392
00:15:02,520 --> 00:15:04,520
along the way, to be sure,

393
00:15:04,520 --> 00:15:06,520
But if we champion the scout oath and law

394
00:15:06,520 --> 00:15:08,520
and we constantly remind

395
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:10,520
our scouts of the context

396
00:15:10,520 --> 00:15:12,520
that they're playing the game in,

397
00:15:12,520 --> 00:15:14,520
things will go well and you'll create

398
00:15:14,520 --> 00:15:16,520
a culture of scouting in

399
00:15:16,520 --> 00:15:18,520
your troop where these questions

